<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joan of Arc</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1412–<a href="/years/1431">1431</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Jeanne d'Arc, Maid of Orléans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joan of Arc was a peasant girl from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domremy-la-pucelle">Domrémy</a>, on the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and Champagne, who believed that saints' voices called her to save <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. In 1429 she helped lift the English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-orleans">siege of Orléans</a> and brought Charles VII to his coronation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>. Captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>, she was condemned for heresy by a pro-English church court and burned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> in 1431; she was canonised in 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc.jpg" alt="Joan of Arc" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joan_of_Arc_miniature_graded.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joan was born c. 1412 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domremy-la-pucelle">Domrémy</a>, a small village in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> valley. Her date of birth is unknown. Her parents were Jacques d'Arc and Isabelle Romée, and her father was a peasant farmer with about 20 hectares of land, who also collected taxes and headed the local watch. Joan was not taught to read and write and dictated her letters.</p><p>She grew up during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>, in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> divided between Armagnacs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> since the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> had the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> assassinated in 1407. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domremy-la-pucelle">Domrémy</a>'s people were loyal to the Armagnac cause. After a raid in 1425 in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> were stolen, Joan had her first vision. She later testified that she saw Saint Michael, and later Saint Margaret and Saint Catherine, and that she swore a vow of virginity to these voices.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Joan met Charles VII at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinon">Chinon</a> in late February or early March 1429, when she was seventeen and he was twenty-six. Theologians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> declared her a good Catholic, and women directed by Yolande of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> verified her virginity. Charles commissioned plate armor for her, and she designed her own banner.</p><p>She reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> on 29 April 1429. At first a figurehead for morale with no formal command, she gained the support of the Armagnac troops and sometimes advised their commanders. On 4 May her arrival rallied soldiers retreating from the bastille of Saint-Loup, and they took it. After the victory at Patay, Charles was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> with Joan at his side. The sieges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in September and La Charité in November failed, and her role in these defeats reduced the court's faith in her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Burgundian troops captured Joan on 23 May 1430, and she was handed to the English in November. Her trial for heresy opened at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> on 9 January 1431 under Bishop Pierre Cauchon. She was accused of wearing men's clothes, of acting on demonic visions and of refusing to submit to the church. The trial was politically motivated: her claims, if unchallenged, would undermine the English claim to the French throne. She was burned at the stake on 30 May 1431.</p><p>A court reinvestigated the trial in 1456 and overturned the verdict, declaring it tainted by deceit and procedural errors. Joan became a national symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. She was canonized in 1920 and, two years later, declared one of the patron saints of France.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joan of Arc” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Joan of Arc</b> came from Domrémy on the borders of Lorraine and Champagne and visited Duke Charles II at Nancy in 1429.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Recovery came with <b>Joan of Arc</b>'s relief of Orléans in 1429 and with Charles VII's reforms, which in 1445 created the compagnies d'ordonnance, the first permanent royal cavalry, and used artillery to win at Formigny in 1450 and Castillon in 1453.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Vichy replaced Marianne with <b>Joan of Arc</b> as the national symbol of France, and its &quot;francisque&quot; insignia arranged a Gallic baton and double-headed hatchet to resemble the fasces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vichy-france">Vichy France · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Joan of Arc</b> entered Orléans with supplies and reinforcements.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-29">29 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>By tradition <b>Joan of Arc</b> was born on Epiphany in the village of Domrémy, in north-eastern France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-6">6 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A church court led by Bishop Pierre Cauchon, an ally of the English, began proceedings against <b>Joan of Arc</b> on charges of heresy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-9">9 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domremy-la-pucelle">Domrémy-la-Pucelle</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.44,5.68&amp;year=1424" aria-label="Domrémy-la-Pucelle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4682 470 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joan of Arc"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5132 740h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5132 740h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.44,5.68&amp;year=1424">What was here in 1424? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If I am not, may God place me there; if I am, may God so keep me. I should be the saddest in all the world if I knew that I were not in the grace of God.&#8221;</p><footer>Trial of Condemnation, 1431 &#183; tr. T. Douglas Murray, 1902 &#183; her answer when asked whether she knew herself to be in the grace of God &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57389/pg57389-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I was thirteen when I had a Voice from God for my help and guidance. The first time that I heard this Voice, I was very much frightened; it was mid-day, in the summer, in my father’s garden.&#8221;</p><footer>Trial of Condemnation, 1431 &#183; tr. T. Douglas Murray, 1902 &#183; her answer to the judges &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57389/pg57389-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Yes, I learnt to spin and to sew; in sewing and spinning I fear no woman in Rouen.&#8221;</p><footer>Trial of Condemnation, 1431 &#183; tr. T. Douglas Murray, 1902 &#183; asked whether she had learnt a trade in her youth &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57389/pg57389-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am come in God’s name; I have nothing to do here; let me be sent back to God, whence I came.&#8221;</p><footer>Trial of Condemnation, 1431 &#183; tr. T. Douglas Murray, 1902 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57389/pg57389-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It was I, myself, who bore this banner, when I attacked the enemy, to save killing any one, for I have never killed any one.&#8221;</p><footer>Trial of Condemnation, 1431 &#183; tr. T. Douglas Murray, 1902 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57389/pg57389-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Better, forty times better, my banner than my sword!&#8221;</p><footer>Trial of Condemnation, 1431, asked whether she loved her banner or her sword more &#183; tr. T. Douglas Murray, 1902 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57389/pg57389-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
